<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-chapter-5" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Gradients, Optimization, and Lagrange Multipliers</title>

<introduction>
  <p>
    In this chapter, we develop tools for understanding how multivariable functions change and how they
    reach extreme values. Central to this study is the gradient, a vector that captures both the direction
    of steepest increase and the rate of change of a function at a point. Gradients provide a powerful
    geometric interpretation of partial derivatives working together.
  </p>

  <p>
    We begin by studying directional derivatives and gradients, emphasizing how they describe local
    behavior of a surface. From there, we examine higher-order partial derivatives and use them to
    classify critical points as maxima, minima, or saddle points. These techniques allow us to analyze
    the shape of a surface and predict its behavior near important points.
  </p>

  <p>
    The chapter concludes with constrained optimization using Lagrange multipliers. This method
    shows how optimal values occur when the geometry of a function aligns with the geometry of a
    constraint. By the end of the chapter, you should be able to solve both unconstrained and constrained
    optimization problems and understand the geometric ideas that justify the methods.
  </p>
</introduction>

